<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Liang dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">502 CE – 557 CE · capital Jiankang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern dynasty of Emperor Wu of Liang, the great Buddhist patron whose half-century of cultural brilliance ended in the catastrophe of Hou Jing's rebellion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Xiao Yan</a>, a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-qi">Southern Qi</a> imperial clan and a noted poet, rebelled against the murderous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-baojuan">Xiao Baojuan</a> in 501, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and in 502 founded the Liang dynasty as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> reigned for 47 years, reforming examinations for officials and promoting learning. A devout Buddhist, he offered himself as a servant to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongtai-temple">Tongtai Temple</a> several times, so that ministers had to ransom him back, and his court encouraged vegetarianism among monks. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-tong">Xiao Tong</a> compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wen-xuan">Wenxuan</a>, the classic anthology of Chinese literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> was among the largest cities in the world.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 548 the defecting general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> rebelled and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> died a captive in 549. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> in 554 and set up <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-liang-555-587">Western Liang</a> (555-587) as a puppet. In 557 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-chen">Chen Baxian</a> deposed the last emperor and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chen-dynasty">Chen dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/liang-dynasty.jpg" alt="Liang dynasty" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liang_dynasty_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>502 CE – 557 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiankang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emperor Wu of Liang, Emperor Jianwen of Liang, Emperor Yuan of Liang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Liang dynasty, Xiao Liang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=530&amp;focus=liang-dynasty" aria-label="Liang dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7737 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Liang dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8057 1267L8057 1267M8057 1267L8057 1267M8057 1267L7920 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8057 1267h0M8057 1267h0M7920 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8057 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jiankang. <a href="/atlas?year=530&amp;focus=liang-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
